1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Before his marriage, Jean-Baptiste had previously been involved "à la façon du pays" [in the style of the country] with a native woman who had borne his children. Marie-Anne was tolerant and accepting of the childr
2 CONC en arising from this previous relationship, although the other woman was jealous and reportedly threatened to poison her. Despite this incident, the Lagimodières generally had good relationships with the Aboriginal peoples they en
2 CONC countered. 
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------
2 CONT http://www.mhs.mb.ca/docs/pageant/10/jeanbaptiste.shtml
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------
2 CONT http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio.php?id_nbr=4014 _______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Baptiste Lagimodiere b. Dec 26 1778 Trois Riviers married Josette (Indian) 1800 they had 3 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Rose Antoinette b.1801 Margerite Lagimodiere b.? Lisette Lagimodiere b.1803
2 CONT
2 CONT Then He married Marie Anne Gaboury Apr 21 1806 they had 8 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Reine Lagimodiere b. Jan 6 1807 Pembina North Dakota 2. Jean-Baptiste ( La Prairies)Lagimodiere b. Aug 15 1808 Fort Des Prairies 3. Josette Marie (La Cypress) Lagimodiere b. 1809 Montagne Cypres (Cypress Hills) 4. Benjamin Lagi
2 CONC modiere b. Dec 11 1811 Pembina North Dakota 5. Apolline (Pauline) Lagimodiere b. 1812 St. Boniface 6. Romain Lagimodiere b. Jan 11 1819 St. Boniface 7. Julie Lagimodiere b. July 23 1822 St. Boniface 8. Joseph Lagimodiere b. Dec 20
2 CONC  1825
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Family tradition has it that Louis Riel was born in a house owned by his maternal grandfather, Jean-Baptiste Lagimodiere.  By the year of Riel's birth, Lagimodiere was a prosperous farmer, land owner of consequence and a prominent
2 CONC  member of the Red River Settlement's bourgoisie.  His name is not found among the ranks of the Metis and French-Canadian dissidents who were rattling the cages of the governing establishment during those years.  Although his was 
2 CONC a most adventuresome past, Lagimodiere was deeplt conservative, a man who believed in the predestined hierarchy of his society, who represented authority for authority's sake.  He was to have a profound influence on his grandson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Riel's grandfather Jean Baptiste was born in December 1778 on the family farm near St. Antoine-sur-Richelieu on the east bank of the St. Lawrence River, 135 miles north of Montreal.  Jean Baptiste was eight years old at the 
2 CONC time of his mother's death.  He and his two siblings were taken under the wing of an aunt.  She lived near Maskinoge, in the diocese of Trois Rivieres, the main commercial centre and the seat of regional government.  At the heart 
2 CONC of this busy town was the governor's mansion, the Ursuline monastery and the Recollets cathederal.  Not surprisingly, the set pieces of Jean Baptiste's character were defference to authority and devotion to church.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the town's inception the fur trade had been Trios Rivieres's raison d'etre.  The precious cargo was shipped from the northern forests down the St. Maurice and warehoused in the town, which in turn distributed supplies to the 
2 CONC traders heading north again.  As the fur business spread farther west, the Trois Rivieres's region became one of the incubators hatching young men - from noblemen's illicit offspring to farmer's sons - who were determined to escap
2 CONC e the narrow confines of their church dominated parishes and turn a quick profit from the plushest of gold - the beaver pelt.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Jean Baptiste Lagimodiere first headed west in 1779 at the age of twenty-one, he probably started out as a voyageur for the North West Company.  If his was a standard contract, he would have signed on for three years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lagimodiere began his carrer at a time when the St Lawrence based fur trade was in the grip of violence and chaos.  The merchant aristocracy of Montreal were engaged in cut throat  competition that featured thefy, murder and a fre
2 CONC e flow of liquor to Indian trappers.  There was , however, one advantage that resulted from this brawl - more workers were needed and higher wages paid.  Jean Baptiste Lagimodiere was one of those who benefited.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1804, however, a truce had finally been called and the fur barons amalgamated into one big enterprise, the mighty North West Company.  Immediately all the extra voyageurs and others who had been hired at the height of the rival
2 CONC ry were considered superflous.  At this point Lagimodiere probably lost his job and began a new career , as a hunter of bison and trapper of beaver attached only loosely to the fur companies, thereby joining the ranks of freemen, 
2 CONC les gens libres - a wild, dangerous, sometimes enchanting existence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family tradition has it that Louis Riel was born in a house owned by h is maternal grandfather, Jean-Baptiste Lagimodiere.  By the year of Ri el's birth, Lagimodiere was a prosperous farmer, land owner of consequ ence and a promin
2 CONC ent member of the Red River Settlement's bourgoisie .  His name is not found among the ranks of the Metis and French-Canad ian dissidents who were rattling the cages of the governing establishm ent during those years.  Although hi
2 CONC s was a most adventuresome past, L agimodiere was deeplt conservative, a man who believed in the predesti ned hierarchy of his society, who represented authority for authority' s sake.  He was to have a profound influence on his g
2 CONC randson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Riel's grandfather Jean Baptiste was born in December 1778 on th e family farm near St. Antoine-sur-Richelieu on the east bank of the S t. Lawrence River, 135 miles north of Montreal.  Jean Baptiste was eig ht years old at t
2 CONC he time of his mother's death.  He and his two siblin gs were taken under the wing of an aunt.  She lived near Maskinoge, i n the diocese of Trois Rivieres, the main commercial centre and the se at of regional government.  At the 
2 CONC heart of this busy town was the gov ernor's mansion, the Ursuline monastery and the Recollets cathederal .  Not surprisingly, the set pieces of Jean Baptiste's character were defference to authority and devotion to church.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the town's inception the fur trade had been Trios Rivieres's rais on d'etre.  The precious cargo was shipped from the northern forests d own the St. Maurice and warehoused in the town, which in turn distribu ted supplies to t
2 CONC he traders heading north again.  As the fur business spread farther west, the Trois Rivieres's region became one of the inc ubators hatching young men - from noblemen's illicit offspring to farm er's sons - who were determined to 
2 CONC escape the narrow confines of thei r church dominated parishes and turn a quick profit from the plushest of gold - the beaver pelt.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Jean Baptiste Lagimodiere first headed west in 1779 at the age o f twenty-one, he probably started out as a voyageur for the North Wes t Company.  If his was a standard contract, he would have signed on fo r three years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lagimodiere began his carrer at a time when the St Lawrence based fur trade was in the grip of violence and chaos.  The merchant aristocrac y of Montreal were engaged in cut throat  competition that featured th efy, murder and a f
2 CONC ree flow of liquor to Indian trappers.  There was , however, one advantage that resulted from this brawl - more workers were needed and higher wages paid.  Jean Baptiste Lagimodiere was one of those who benefited.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1804, however, a truce had finally been called and the fur barons a malgamated into one big enterprise, the mighty North West Company.  Im mediately all the extra voyageurs and others who had been hired at th e height of the ri
2 CONC valry were considered superflous.  At this point Lag imodiere probably lost his job and began a new career , as a hunter o f bison and trapper of beaver attached only loosely to the fur compani es, thereby joining the ranks of fre
2 CONC emen, les gens libres - a wild, da ngerous, sometimes enchanting existence.
