2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/f6/da/a4/bd/53444864b9fff7e6/img_8789_original.jpg?hash=2acdeb953e26ffa73a01766b596cdbafcf7e663f896eb0000a9aba49527862ac.1797062399
2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE “ 504/2284 Clinton Street, Black Bottom, and what is now Elmwood Park Detroit. Also known as the Henry Poupard/Joseph Monfins/Josephine Monfils and later on as the Fred Burnsides residence built 1874-75? Henry was listed as living
3 CONC  in this home as early as 1875 and lived in this home for a short time working as a Butcher at one point. This home would notably I believe be occupied on and off by the Monfins family who I believe were Canadian-French, with Jose
3 CONC ph being a Carpenter. Throughout the 1890s and early 1900s this home was occupied by John Murphy who was a Machinist. And other names such as Phillip J. Beaubein also occupied this home as well during this time period. At some poi
3 CONC nt, Joseph Monfins Daughter Josephine occupied this home with her family and according to the 1920 directories, she was a Dressmaker. By 1940 this was home to Fred Burnsides along with Amos Edwards. Amos if reading the census corr
3 CONC ectly was the Son in Law of Fred Burnsides. Around 16 people lived in this home at the time and they were all family members of Fred Burnsides. Fred and his wife Hazel were from South Carolina and at some point came to Detroit dur
3 CONC ing the Great Migration and was listed as working as a Crane Operator during this time. The Burnsides family were still living in this home by the 1950s, and according to the census Fred and Hazel's two eldest children were born i
3 CONC n South Carolina while the remaining children were born in Michigan. Sadly this home would eventually be Demolished sometime in the 1960s for what is now Elmwood Park. Credit goes to the Burton Historical Collection for the image.
3 CONC ” Mark Connett II, Facebook, Historical Detroit Area Architecture, December 14, 2023
1 CHAN
2 DATE 14 DEC 2023
3 TIME 14:16:55
