1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ephriam MEILLEUR
2 CONT
2 CONT Veteran of 42 years in the railway business before retiring in January, last year, Ephriam Isaac Meilleur, of Minnow Lake, died in St. Joseph's Hospital, Sudbury, Monday, in his 68th year.
2 CONT
2 CONT General yardmaster with the CPR in Sudbury since 1943, he had been living at the home of a daughter, Mrs. B. J. Dugas, 18 Manor Rd. Minnow Lake.
2 CONT
2 CONT The body is resting at Jackson and Barnard Funeral Home, Larch St., Sudbury. Solemn requiem mass will be sung in the Church of Christ the King 9 o'clock Thursday morning. Internment will be in the Sudbury Roman Catholic cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born, raised and educated at Wiley, Ont., son of the late Mr. and Mrs Joseph Meilleur, he worked in lumber camps before beginning his railroad career as a yardman in North Bay in 1907.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next year he moved to Sudbury and rose steadily through the ranks. In 1912, he was made yard foreman at Sudbury. Eight years later, he was appointed yardmaster and in 1943 became general yardmaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the busiest times in Sudbury yards recalled by Meilleur was when the King and Queen visited the city in 1939. There were 27 special trains operated that day, one of them running 26 coaches.
2 CONT
2 CONT The veteran railroader often recalled that when he arrived in Sudbury to work on switching, he was paid 19 cents an hour for a 10-hour day.
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the Brotherhood of Railway Trainmen, he was also a member of the Knights of Columbus and an ardent baseball and hockey fan. He also attended the Church of Christ the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fond of hunting and fishing, he followed these pastimes in his earlier days as well as playing on CPR hockey and baseball teams.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married April 5, 1907, in Pembroke to Christina Hudson, who survives. Also surviving are four daughters, Mrs. E. McAvoy, of Sudbury ; Mrs. B. J. Dugas, of Minnow Lake ; Teresa and Joan, at home ; and five sons, Raymond, Ear
2 CONC l, Lawrence, Foch and Frederick, all of Sudbury ; 18 grand-children, and four sisters, Mrs. Ben Lance, of Wiley ; Mrs. Mary Romaine, of Pembroke ; Mrs. R. Stewart, of Deux Rivieres, and Mrs. Ben Dufoe, of Wiley ; and three brother
2 CONC s, John, of Wiley ; August, of Stonecliffe, Ont., and Hector, of Rapides des Joachims, Que.
