2 CONT {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\cocoartf1404\cocoasubrtf470{\fonttbl\f0\fswiss\fcharset0 Helvetica;\f1\fnil\fcharset0 MicrosoftSansSerif;}{\colortbl;\red255\green255\blue255;}\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx
2 CONC 5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0\f0\fs24 \cf0 Witnessed by Francisco Ferlan and Maria Mercier  \par \pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0\f1\fs17 \cf0 Alternate Spelling "Necterre"   \par Nick-name "Victor"
2 CONC   \par   \par Phoebe Lachapelle Notes:  \par The Lachapel de Lorglue family migrated to the Province of Quebec in Canada at an unknown date.  Quebec was quite developed by that time, Great-grandfather's family lived in Saint Agnes
2 CONC s.  They came to Baltic, Connecticut to work in the textile mill, brought there by some textile manufacturers at a time when those kind of mills were sprouting all over New England.  Grandpa's wife was expecting at the time and hi
2 CONC s dad had come to the States with them.  Dad was born in Baltic in 1875.  Two years later another child was expected and Dad's mother was very sick and wanted to go back home to Canada.  Then Uncle Wilfred was born and his mom (Vi
2 CONC rgina Serazin) died of flebitis.  \par Dad's grandfather died when Dad was five years old.  \par Dad's father's name was Nectaire Lachapelle and his mom's name Virginia "nee Seracin." Dad's fathe married again to a woman whose las
2 CONC t name was Bonin, first name unknown.  One son and three daughters were born to that couple: Micahel, Mary Louise, Elizabeth, and grandpa's wife died at Diana's birth.  \par Grandpa married again to a Mary Chase and they had one c
2 CONC hild, Albert.  \par All of the child have passed away except my Uncle Mitchel (Micahel) who is ninety two or three, still strong and healthy with a voice like a fog horn and is able to attend wakes and get around.  He lives by him
2 CONC self in an apartment under one of his daughter's family.  \par   \par Re-translated by Jim Medeiros:  \par The Lachapel de Lorglue family migrated to the Province of Quebec in Canada at an unknown date.  Quebec was quite developed
2 CONC  by that time, Nectaire Lachapelle's family lived in Saint Agness.  They came to Baltic, Connecticut to work in the textile mill, brought there by some textile manufacturers at a time when those kind of mills were sprouting all ov
2 CONC er New England.  Grandma Virginia Seracin Lachapelle was expecting at the time and great grandpa Louis had come to the States with them.  My dad, John Fulgence Lachapelle was born in Baltic in 1875.  Two years later another child 
2 CONC was expected and Dad's mother was very sick and wanted to go back home to Canada.  Wilfred was born and his mom (Virgina Serazin) died of flebitis. (Jim's note: Wilfred was born in Canada in 1876 and another Son, Joseph Adolphe, w
2 CONC as born in 1878.  Virginia died in 1879 in St-Michel-des-Saints, where Wilfred and Adolphe were born)  \par Dad's grandfather died when Dad was five years old.  \par John Fulgences Lachapelle's father's name was Nectaire Lachapell
2 CONC e and his mom's name Virginia "nee Seracin." Dad's father married again to a woman whose last name was Bonin, first name unknown.  One son and three daughters were born to that couple: Micahel, Mary Louise, Elizabeth, and grandpa'
2 CONC s wife died at Diana's birth.  \par Grandpa married again to a Mary Chase and they had one child, Albert.  \par All of the child have passed away except my Uncle Mitchel (Micahel) who is ninety two or three, still strong and healt
2 CONC hy with a voice like a fog horn and is able to attend wakes and get around.  He lives by himself in an apartment under one of his daughter's family.  \par   \par   \par   \par The Baltic Mills Complex, covering 16.58 acres, was at
2 CONC   \par one time the largest textile mill in the United States.  \par Initiated in 1890 and owned by the former Governor of  \par Rhode Island, it consisted of several buildings, the last of  \par which was constructed in the 1920s
2 CONC . The buildings were  \par destroyed by fire in 1999, with the exception of Building  \par #10, which covers 54,000sf on 4 floors and is constructed  \par of granite block. Utilities are not on site at this time, but  \par are on 
2 CONC adjacent properties and readily connected. The  \par entire site and surrounding Village/mill housing is listed on  \par the National Register of Historic Places. It lies in the heart  \par of downtown, and has several hundred fee
2 CONC t of frontage on  \par the Shetucket Riv  \par   \par By Jim Medeiros:  Buried at Sacred Heart Church Cemetery (Refered to as the "old Church")  in a lot that only bears his last wife's name Marie St. Cyr. - who died before Nectar
2 CONC  in 1926.  Lot 122C in the cemetery.\f0\fs24  \par \pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0\cf0 \{\\rtf1\\ansi\\ansicpg1252\\cocoartf1187\\cocoasubrtf
