1 NAME Francois Xavier /Cyriac dit Aphoral Taylor/
2 GIVN Francois Xavier
2 SURN Cyriac dit Aphoral Taylor
2 _MARNM Cyriac dit Aphoral Taylor
2 NICK Tiriac
2 NICK Syriac
2 NICK Taylor
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BEF 20 JUL 1835
2 PLAC New York
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 MAY 1862
2 PLAC Redford, Clinton Co., New York
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE 18 MAY 1862
2 ADDR
3 STAE Clinton Co.
3 CTRY New York
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT No doubt - you have the right people!
2 CONT
2 CONT Pontbriand's Clinton County marriages -
2 CONT
2 CONT Francois Cyriac (min) married Celina Bellavance on 24 Sep 1855 atRedford. He s/o Frederic Cyriac and Emilie Normandin She d/o Joseph Bellavance and Eudoxie Vermet
2 CONT
2 CONT Bellavance/Bellevance/Bellevence/etc. is a dit name forGagne/Gagnier/Gagnon/etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was also a marriage for Francois' brother:
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederic Cyriac married Philomene Vertefueille on 6 Jul 1856 atRedford. Her parents were Olivier Vertefueille and Catherine Dandurand
2 CONT
2 CONT The death records show the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT Francois Cyriac, epouse de Celine Gagne, died 17, buried 18 May 1862at Redford at the age of 26. Witnesses were Pierre Pare and JosephGagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Pare was a churchwarden and shows up in majority of burialrecords, so I don't know if there is any connection with the familyother than in that capacity. Joseph Gagne is probably Celine's father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Celine/Celina/Cecilia are also interchangeable. Frank is Anglo forFrancois/Francis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another entry:
2 CONT
2 CONT Emilie Syriac, age 76, no spouse shown, was buried on 25 Mar 1880 atRedford. Witnesses were Frederic Syriac and Philemon Coulombe.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is undoubtedly Emilie Normandin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Let me know if I can check anything else out for you.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bev
2 CONT
2 CONT Exerpts from The Battle of Cedar Creek:
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Merrit had pushed Kidd forward yet another time towardWoodstock to see if Lomax still held the town. Kidd's advance elementsencountered some of Lomax's boys just south of the village, but aftersome spirited but brief sk
2 CONC irmishing, Kidd fell back to Tom's Brook.(Battle of Toms Brook) Farther to the west, Kidd's Michigan Brigade fanned out to connectwith Custer's left flank. Captain Joseph Martin's 6th New York LightBattery accompanied Kidd while L
2 CONC ieut. Frank E. Taylor's 1st US LightBattery marched with Lowell. (Battle of Tom's Brook)
2 CONT
2 CONT Custer now began his final push......... Pennington's Brigade likewisesurged across the stream to the east, only this time, Lieut. Col.George A. Purington's 2nd Ohio spread out to the left and, joiningwith Kidd's brigade, began to
2 CONC  lap up and around the Laurel Brigade'sright.............The "Laurels" were doing all they could to hold offPennington, let alone deal with Kidd who was now moving around theirright. Johnson's withdrawal farther to the east had pe
2 CONC rmitted theWolverines to penetrate the gap between the two Rebel horse divisionsand the Michiganders, supported by the fire of Martin's Battery, werebent on reaching the Confederate rear. (Battle of Toms Brook)
2 CONT
2 CONT Before daylight the guard shook the now drowsy Kidd from his slumberand informed him that scattered shots had been heard coming fromCupp's Ford. Kidd at once ordered his men awakened and the regimentsformed, but cautioned his offi
2 CONC cers to do so without usingbugles........In short order, Kidd had his regiments formed and readyto move out.......Merritt soon galloped up and immediately orderedKidd to take his brigade to the support of the 7th Michigan at Cupp'
2 CONC sFord...........The 7th Michigan put up a good fight but lost a numberof it's men killed and wounded as they were driven back a shortdistance from the creek. By this time, however, Kidd's remainingregiments were coming rapidly to 
2 CONC the Seventh's aid. Within ahalf-hour, they had advanced upon Owen's lodgment and, after some"hard fighting," had driven the Rebels back across the ford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Farther to the left, Martin's 6th New York Light Artillery opened firefrom near Kidd's Brigade. Martin's guns were shielded, deployed on thereverse slope of a small hillock with only their muzzles showing overthe crest. The Union 
2 CONC artillery and Carter's Rebel guns.....soon beganto duel in earnest. The Yankee gunners fought well but the Rebelartillery fire increased in volume and accuracy as Carter pushed moreof his batteries to the front.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon the Federal artillery began to open on the Confederate lines.Gibb's Buckeye battery........joined Merritt's lighter horse batteriesin supporting the Union attack northeast of Middletown...........Owingto these losses, the Fed
2 CONC eral artillery was only able to put sometwenty guns into action in the afternoon.
2 CONT
