2 CONT From “The Saranac Valley” by Sarah Baker     The first church spire to embellish the Redford landscape rose from alog chapel erected in 1837 by a small group of Catholics. It was thatof Notre Dame Church of the Assumption of the B
2 CONC lessed Virgin Mary.These few Catholics were at that time a minority group in the hamletof Redford, and having been refused the use of the school house as aplace to worship, decided to construct a building of their own.Previously, 
2 CONC Mass was said in the homes of Patrick Meaghan and ThomasLeonard by Rev. F. F. Rogers or Rev. Rooney of St. John’s Church, whooccasionally visited them. For the little chapel, a lot three rods square was purchased fromPeter Strack 
2 CONC and a frame building, 32 x 24 ft. was built from handhewn lumber. With most of the materials being donated, the othernecessities were met from subscriptions ranging from $5.00 to $50.00from the ten Catholic families located there.
2 CONC      Felix O’Neil, James Madden, Peter Strack     Thomas Leonard, Patrick Moore, Louis Pepin     John Quickly, Charles Graves, John Vient     John Pepin     The number of Catholics in the community increased until in 1853, whenthe
2 CONC  Oblate Fathers of Mary Immaculate (who had charge of the Frenchpeople of Plattsburgh and outside Missions), took over the littleRedford Mission. there were sixty families. A larger church buildingstarted at this time was opened b
2 CONC y Father Sallaz saying thebenediction on August 15. 1855, a date that is still the occasion foran annual celebration known as the "Redford Picnic." It was later tobe seen that this was only a beginning in the development of theCat
2 CONC holic religion in Redford.     From “Annals of Notre Dame Church of the Assumption of the BlessedVirgin Mary” by Reverend R. C. Laramee,(1931), we find thisinformation:     "The much honored Father C.F.M. Sallaz, was born in Franc
2 CONC e in 1832,entered the Oblate Fathers Congregation at Merseille, France in 1850,Missionary in Canada from 1851 to 1853. and at Plattsburgh from thatyear until 1872. He died at Buffalo on February 9, 1873. May thispious and zealous 
2 CONC missionary, founder of the Catholic Congregation ofRedford. continue to live in the memory and prayers of itsinhabitants, where he has labored so faithfully for the glory of Godand His holy Church."     "I have portions of a histo
2 CONC rical sketch of the parish of Our Lady ofthe Assumption, in Redford, by Father E.C. Laramee who was pastorthere from 1896 until 1932. According to him the first inhabitantswere French Canadians "who cut their way through the fores
2 CONC t towardsthe end of the third decade of this century". ( More correctly it wasat the start of the third decade.) He indicated that at the time ofthe publication of his little history in 1899 "Redford is a village ofthree hundred s
2 CONC ouls situated on the North side of the Saranac, mostlybuilt in the upland, a little up from the river. In 1831 and 1832 asawmill and gristmill were built, and about the same time a glassfactory which enjoyed a precarious existence
2 CONC , and was finallyabandoned in 1852. " Some of the glass makers belonged to the Corningfamily, and Redford glass is rare and precious. Father Laramee statesthat Redford at that time had one hotel and two blacksmith's shops.And he s
2 CONC ays that one of the reasons why French Canadians moved intothe Saranac region was to work in the iron mines and forges thatflourished in the area until 1886. In one year the Catholic populationof Redford grew from 60 families to 6
2 CONC 00, four-fifths of them havingcome from Canada. But from 1886 there was no more industry in thearea. About 318 families, i.e., 1658 people inhabited Redford than.The lack of jobs from the late 1880's probably created an exodus fro
2 CONC mthe region.         
