1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1927 newspaper article from Larry Bodoh: With the death of Mrs. Louise Trombley, aged 82, which occurred at the home of her niece, Mrs. Joseph Bessett, Dear Creek, April 7, of this year, there passed an ancestor of
2 CONC  five generations. The above picture taken a short time ago is that of Mrs. Trombley, 82, her daughter Mrs. George Whitcomb, 62, granddaughter, Mrs. Henry Roberts, aged 41, great granddaughter, Mrs. Fred Lachance, aged 22, and gre
2 CONC at great granddaughter, Loretta, five months. Mrs. Trombley's death was due to an illness of cancer and has been confined to her bed three months before her death. During her illness she was cared for by her niece, Mrs. Bessett. D
2 CONC eceased was born in the state of New York, December 12, 1844, the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Mitchell Bodah. When she was five years old she came with her parents to Fond Du Lac, living there until a few years after her marriage to 
2 CONC Peter Surprise, which occurred in 1860. The family lived for a short time in Suamico after leaving Fond Du Lac and finally located in Gladstone. Mich. Following an accident in which Mr. Surprise lost his arm, the burden of support
2 CONC ing six children fell on the mother, who toiled unceasingly as daily the early pioneers did. A number of years ago Mr. Surprise passed away and in 1909, Mrs. Trombly married Fear Trombley in Gladstone, Mich. The following ten year
2 CONC s they lived in Gladstone until the death of her second husband which occurred in 1919. Since that time she has lived with her daughter, Mrs. Telbet until July 18, 1926 when she came to live with her niece. Mrs. Joseph Bessett in 
2 CONC Deer Creek. Survivors are six children: Mrs. George Whitcomb of Green Bay, Mrs. Thomas Trudeau of Forky, Wash, Mrs. Alice Telbet of Forky, Wash, Mrs. Della LaLand of Osier, Mich, Marshall Surprise of Escanaba, Mich, and Thomas Sur
2 CONC prise of Florida, MN.
