1 NOTE {geni:about_me} rom Free Press; Dec. 18, 1924; Andre Nault, Veteran of Riel Times, Dead. Discoverer of Trespassing Surveyors, Cause of Uprising, Was 95 Years Old Bore Mark of Rifle Butt in Skull -- Large Family in Greater Winnipeg
2 CONC  Andre Nault, aged 95 years, passed on to the great beyond last night at 8 o'clock. With him goes one of the few remaining links between Winnipeg and the earliest days of Fort Garry. He was born at Point Douglas in 1829, son of Am
2 CONC able Nault and Josephte Lagimodiere, the daughter of Jean Baptiste Lagimodiere, the famous Red river settler, who travelled from Fort Garry to Montreal by Pembina on snowshoes in order to notify Lord Selkirk that the Indians were 
2 CONC threatening the lives of the settlers. Mr. Nault was 10 years of age when the 'Treaty of Liberty' was signed. The passage of that treaty had been forced by Louis Riel, Sr., the father of the famous Louis Riel. The commencement of 
2 CONC the Red river troubles came with Mr. Nault discovering English surveyors measuring his land. He returned home and notified Louis Riel, who was living a short distance away. The surveyors were told that they had no right to measure
2 CONC  the lands and were ordered off. They left the premises. In reference to the rising led by Riel, Mr. Nault said, 'It was not rebellion, and I do not wish it to be called so. All we desired was justice, our rights, and that the Dom
2 CONC inion government should consult us in reference to the nature of the government in the colony.' When 54 years of age, Mr. Nault was left for dead near Emerson in the 1870 trouble, having been struck on the head with the butt of a 
2 CONC soldier's rifle. He received a gash in his head that would admit the side of the hand. The cavity remains. Year in Prison With the arrival of Wolsely, Mr. Nault escaped to the United States. He was two years in exile and, because 
2 CONC his wife would not cross the line, came home. He was taken prisoner and remained one year in jail with the late Alexander Lepine, who had been arrestied previously. 'I will never forget the year I spent in prison,' he remarked to 
2 CONC a Free Press reporter some time ago. 'That I, who upheld justice, right and the British flag should have been imprisoned by the servants of that same great flag.'
